{"name":"Farmhouse Ochre","hex":"#bd8339","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"farmhouse","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A house (usually the main house) on a farm; thus:\n A farmer's residence.\n\nA house that was once a farmer's residence albeit today lived in by residents whose occupation is not farming.","A farmer's residence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/farmhouse"},{"term":"ochre","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A clay earth pigment containing silica, aluminum and ferric oxide.","A somewhat dark yellowish orange colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a yellow-orange colour.","Referring to cultures that covered their dead with ochre."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ochre"}],"entries":[{"title":"Farmhouse","blocks":[{"text":"A farmhouse is a building that serves as the primary quarters in a rural or agricultural setting. Historically, farmhouses were often combined with space for animals called a housebarn. Other farmhouses may be connected to one or more barns, built to form a courtyard, or with each farm building separate from each other."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Einsiedeln_IMG_2819.JPG/330px-Einsiedeln_IMG_2819.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farmhouse","wikidata":"Q489357"},{"title":"Ochre","blocks":[{"text":"Ochre is a family of natural clay earth pigments, made up of ferric oxide and varying amounts of clay and sand, ranging in colour from yellow to deep orange or brown. The term is also used for the colours produced by this pigment, especially a light brownish-yellow. A variant of ochre containing a large amount of hematite, or dehydrated iron oxide, has a reddish tint known as red ochre. The term \"ochre\" is colloquially used to cover a range of different substances used for their colours in Aboriginal Australian art and ceremonial decoration, for instance the clay known as \"white ochre\" or \"pipe clay\" is derived from kaolin or gypsum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Hellocker-_Pigment.JPG/330px-Hellocker-_Pigment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ochre","wikidata":"Q12353401"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/The_Devil%27s_Farmhouse_in_Mellieha%2C_Malta.jpeg/500px-The_Devil%27s_Farmhouse_in_Mellieha%2C_Malta.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/The_Devil%27s_Farmhouse_in_Mellieha%2C_Malta.jpeg/1280px-The_Devil%27s_Farmhouse_in_Mellieha%2C_Malta.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/The_Devil%27s_Farmhouse_in_Mellieha%2C_Malta.jpeg/500px-The_Devil%27s_Farmhouse_in_Mellieha%2C_Malta.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Devil's Farmhouse in Mellieħa, Malta, built by the Order of St. John with limestone","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Devil's_Farmhouse_in_Mellieha%2C_Malta.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Farmhouse_upper_Trenta_Soca_06052006_63.jpg/250px-Farmhouse_upper_Trenta_Soca_06052006_63.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Farmhouse_upper_Trenta_Soca_06052006_63.jpg/500px-Farmhouse_upper_Trenta_Soca_06052006_63.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Farmhouse_upper_Trenta_Soca_06052006_63.jpg/250px-Farmhouse_upper_Trenta_Soca_06052006_63.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","l